WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.920
One protein has two jobs.

2
00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:06.300
Which job did the cell miss when losing it slowed division?

3
00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:12.200
Chapter one's rule can help: a result chooses only where the twins disagree.

4
00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.780
Its last test put the protein back, and division recovered.

5
00:00:20.083 --> 00:00:21.643
The protein is clathrin.

6
00:00:22.823 --> 00:00:25.803
One protein can do more than one task in a cell.

7
00:00:27.163 --> 00:00:29.783
Clathrin's best-known job is endocytosis.

8
00:00:31.223 --> 00:00:36.183
Clathrin coats small pockets of the cell's surface membrane, which pinch inward and

9
00:00:36.183 --> 00:00:37.643
carry material into the cell.

10
00:00:38.667 --> 00:00:44.627
In 2005, Stephen Royle and colleagues silenced clathrin in human cells in a dish.

11
00:00:46.067 --> 00:00:49.567
Mitosis, the division of one cell into two, took longer.

12
00:00:50.787 --> 00:00:52.487
So clathrin matters for division.

13
00:00:53.887 --> 00:00:54.887
But how?

14
00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:56.840
Here are the twins again.

15
00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:01.120
In the teal twin, clathrin works in mitosis itself.

16
00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:07.360
In the orange twin, it helps only through endocytosis, and division suffers

17
00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:13.360
indirectly. Interference works over a few days, so an indirect route would have time

18
00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:14.360
to build.

19
00:01:14.933 --> 00:01:16.893
Now the rescue from chapter one.

20
00:01:18.093 --> 00:01:23.313
Silence clathrin in both twins, then put back the whole protein, in a form the

21
00:01:23.313 --> 00:01:24.593
interference cannot touch.

22
00:01:25.793 --> 00:01:29.573
If mitosis returns to normal, does that choose between the twins?

23
00:01:35.267 --> 00:01:41.687
It cannot. The whole protein brings back both jobs, so both twins predict rescue.

24
00:01:42.927 --> 00:01:46.567
And in the experiment, full-length clathrin did rescue mitosis.

25
00:01:47.907 --> 00:01:50.527
That strongly ties the defect to clathrin loss.

26
00:01:51.907 --> 00:01:53.627
Which job, rescue cannot say.

27
00:01:54.617 --> 00:01:57.557
They also put back only the front part of clathrin.

28
00:01:58.737 --> 00:01:59.737
It did not rescue.

29
00:02:00.817 --> 00:02:02.897
But a failed rescue is hard to read.

30
00:02:04.137 --> 00:02:08.517
The piece might be made at the wrong level, fold badly, or sit in the wrong place.

31
00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.880
To make the twins disagree, change the protein itself.

32
00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:17.920
Build a resistant copy that keeps one job and loses the other.

33
00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:21.320
This is a separation-of-function mutant.

34
00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:25.780
First show that one job still works and the other does not.

35
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.460
Then check its amount, folding and location.

36
00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:34.260
Royle and Leon Lagnado built such a copy the next year.

37
00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:37.140
What does each twin predict for it?

38
00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:40.280
That is a question for you in the text below.

39
00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:43.140
Work it out before you read on.

40
00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:48.870
So rescue answers one question: did losing the protein cause the defect?

41
00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:54.270
To ask which job, use a copy that keeps one and loses the other.

42
00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:58.210
Before you test it, write down what each twin predicts.

43
00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:03.310
Rescue supports the protein's role; split its jobs to learn which.
